// Solution — Module 01, Exercise 01: Fixed-Width Integers
//
// Task 1 & 2 are about what happens when a literal does not fit.
// Note the wording in the exercise: these are *warnings*, not errors.
// crunch-c wraps the value the way C does and tells you it did.
//
//   u8(256)  -> spooked: u8(): 256 does not fit u8 [0, 255] - wrapped to 0
//   i8(200)  -> spooked: i8(): 200 does not fit i8 [-128, 127] - wrapped to -56
//
// 200 wraps to -56 because 200 - 256 = -56: the bit pattern 0b11001000 read
// as a signed byte is negative. The bits never changed, only how we read them.

// Task 1: a u8 literal that overflows.
over_u8 = u8(256)
print(f"u8(256) wrapped to: {over_u8}")

// Task 2: a signed i8 literal that overflows.
over_i8 = i8(200)
print(f"i8(200) wrapped to: {over_i8}")

// Task 3: a u16 at its maximum.
max_u16 = u16(65535)
print(f"u16 max: {max_u16}")

// Task 4: an i64 holding a number no 32-bit type could.
large = i64(9999999999)
print(f"i64 large: {large}")

// Task 5: the same number does not fit an i32, whose range stops just above
// 2.1 billion. It wraps rather than saturating - you do not get "the biggest
// i32", you get whatever the low 32 bits happen to spell, read as signed.
// Guessing this one correctly in advance is hard, and that is the point: a
// value that overflowed is not a value you can reason about from the
// original number.
too_big = i32(9999999999)
print(f"i32(9999999999) wrapped to: {too_big}")
